A||||9||1FieroGTRacingInc||cooling fan's||||5||
Z||000000||1FieroGTRacingInc||12-11-2000||02:16 AM||||Those small cooling fan's that supposed to cool the coil, and the alternator... are they really nessesary???  Do they do that much good??  I'm just trying to free up space and lose those tubes sticking out toward the engine...  thank's      Steve||||reg||5
Z||000001||Oreif||12-11-2000||02:22 AM||||I don't think they are all that important since Pontiac removed them from the 1988 V-6's. ||||reg||1
Z||000002||1FieroGTRacingInc||12-11-2000||01:51 PM||||Thank's Orief...<BR>I didn't know that they were *eliminated* in 88. Since I want to take mine off and out also , Do I need to remove the actual fan's too??  Do I just leave a hole there? Just remove the pipes??   Thanks   Steve||||reg||1
Z||000003||Oreif||12-11-2000||03:00 PM||||Removing the fan will remove weight. Not enough to make a difference. If you want just cut the tubes down and the fan will still circulate air.||||reg||1
Z||000004||1FST2M6||12-11-2000||03:00 PM||||take the pipes out.. a 10mm wrench and flat head screw driver.. and pull.. the duct work from the fan will then come out.. the fan has 3 big philips screw in it.. on the back side of the fan.. take the 48mm nuts out of the fan cover and pull it back you'll see the 2 screw you couldn't get too before.. and unplug it throw it away... place a piece of duct tape over the exposed plug.. you can use these wires for an MSD igniton later! free switched power! you'll still need a stronger lead but you'll have the switched access.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000005||1FieroGTRacingInc||12-11-2000||11:59 PM||||Heeeyyy  That's a great idea....  Thank's guy's    steve||||reg||1
Z||000006||WKDFIRO||12-12-2000||07:29 AM||||88 V6s also had a heat covering that completely enclosed the connector pipe to block the heat from the coil and if I remembered correctly went with a smaller alternator casing to put a shield on the manifold. <P>Oddly enough the 88 connector pipes were also the only ones known to crack...<P>I kept my cooling tubes on since I just don't want to find out how important my cooling tubes are out in the middle of no where... <P>Turn on your AC with your trunk lid up and feel how much air is being blown on the coil. Its a pretty good breeze if you are sitting still in traffic.<p>[This message has been edited by WKDFIRO (edited 12-12-2000).]||||reg||4
Z||000007||theogre||12-12-2000||07:50 PM||||Whatever cooling that fan provides, keep it. The engine compartment temp in Fiero can easily reach 200F. I stuck a thermometer in there once and kept an eye on it as I drove around. (Actually my pasenger watched it. It was too small for me to read and drive.) The temp was 150+ on a 70F day driving around at 30-40mph. It started climbing higher yet as soon as I parked. If you don't beleive me get one of those little pocket digital meat thermometers that goes to 300F at a grocery store or K/Wal-mart. Stick it between the deck lid and the vent grate.<P>The alternator and coil may not fail outright but they will loose more and more efficiency as they heat up. As wire heats it gains resistance. Alternators and coils are nothing but coils of wire inside. A traditional alternator is only considered about 50% efficient to start with....<P>I wouln't read a whole lot into Pontiac deleting the cooler in 88. It was just as likely a cost cutting issue as anything else. Car makers regularly sacrifice things to cost that effect reliabilty and/or safety. (Did you think Ford leaving out the plastic gas tank shield was an accident? GM's refusal to move the fuel tanks on it's trucks ranks right in there to.)<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 12-12-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||1FieroGTRacingInc||12-12-2000||08:11 PM||||THEOGRE,<BR>Thank's for the advise... pretty good advise at that too....lol   Good enough for me to leave them in there...  Thank's   Steve||||reg||1
Z||000009||rogergarrison||12-13-2000||11:15 PM||||Ive got a Ferrari kit on my 86 SE. I left the fan and tubes in mine, especially since i have a turbo. Ive blew out 2 OEM coils over 3 years, not ive got an ACCEL. I also got Autozone lifetime warranty alternator because the burn out every year and a half or so.(im expert at changing them now, can do in 15 min) Ferrari rear deck/engine cover is 1/2 covered by louvers from rear window to almost 18" back. In the summer I could use it for a portable barbeque grill lol. Only makes hot engine compartment though, engine never gets over 180* internally. I have seen some kit guys put electric fans on the underside of lid too. (I still wonder why GM ever bothered to put rear defroster in Fiero anyway-rear glass is only like 5 inches from exhaust manifold)||||reg||1
